  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Quadrilateral $ABCD$ has vertices $A(2,-2)$, $B(5,2)$, $C(9,-1)$ and $D(6,-5)$. Show that the diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ are perpendicular.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$m_{AC}=\\frac17$, $m_{BD}=-7$. Since $m_{AC}m_{BD}=-1$, $AC\\perp BD$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m_{AC}&=\\frac{-1-(-2)}{9-2}\\\\m_{AC}&=\\frac17\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}m_{BD}&=\\frac{-5-2}{6-5}\\\\m_{BD}&=-7\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}m_{AC}m_{BD}&=\\frac17\\times(-7)\\\\m_{AC}m_{BD}&=-1\\end{align*}$$\nThe product of the gradients is $-1$, so $AC\\perp BD$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
